---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="Scope & Ethics" description="Authorized-use scope and ethics for LolChain.">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="crumb"><a href="/">LolChain</a> / SCOPE &amp; ETHICS</div>
    <section class="hero" style="padding:30px 0 10px">
      <div class="label kicker">Scope &amp; ethics</div>
      <h1 style="font-size:clamp(28px,4.5vw,44px)">Authorized use only.</h1>
    </section>

    <article class="prose">
      <div class="callout">
        <strong>LolChain is a conceptual reference, not an attack toolkit.</strong> Entries
        describe how one weakness hands capabilities to the next — the <em>handoff</em>,
        its outcome, detection, and remediation. They are not copy-paste weaponized
        exploit code. Use the ideas only where you are authorized to.
      </div>

      <h2>Who this is for</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Penetration testers &amp; red teams</strong> — reason about where a
          low-severity finding actually leads, and prioritise accordingly.</li>
        <li><strong>Defenders &amp; blue teams</strong> — see which single link to break;
          a chain dies at its weakest step.</li>
        <li><strong>Engineers &amp; educators</strong> — understand how real breaches are
          assembled from ordinary bugs.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Rules of engagement</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Only test systems you <strong>own</strong> or have <strong>explicit written
          authorization</strong> to assess, within the agreed scope.</li>
        <li>Follow responsible/coordinated disclosure for anything you find.</li>
        <li>Respect the law and the rules of any bug-bounty or engagement contract.</li>
        <li>This site provides no access, no credentials, and no exploit payloads.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Defensive parity</h2>
      <p>
        Every node carries <code>detection</code> and <code>remediation</code>. The same
        graph that shows an attack path shows defenders where to cut it. Breaking one
        capability handoff breaks every chain that depends on it — fix the shared link
        and you kill many paths at once.
      </p>

      <div class="callout good">
        <strong>Derived chains are suggestions, not facts.</strong> Edges the build
        infers from capability tags are shown as <em>derived · unverified</em>. They are
        plausible handoffs to investigate — verify before relying on any of them.
      </div>

      <h2>Attribution</h2>
      <p>
        Curated chains cite their sources (PortSwigger, OWASP, vendor advisories,
        public research). References are never invented. If you spot a wrong or missing
        citation, please open a correction.
      </p>

      <h2>No warranty</h2>
      <p>
        The information here is provided as-is for lawful, authorized security work and
        education. The maintainers accept no liability for misuse.
      </p>

      <p style="margin-top:28px"><a href="/about/">→ How LolChain's model works</a></p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
